WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.730
Here is an extra fast, closed cast-on.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.800
Now, it may not be the prettiest cast-on

3
00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:12.220
you can find, but it is closed and it
will get your knitting started quickly.

4
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.300
It uses slip.

5
00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:18.220
I'm showing it on a Brother
machine using the part buttons.

6
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.140
On Silver/Singer machines,

7
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:25.440
set the side levers to slip
and you'll get the same results.

8
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.240
Put into work the number of needles needed

9
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.580
and then pull every other
needle all the way out.

10
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.520
Let's call these the even number needles.

11
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.580
Set your carriage to slip.

12
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.220
On my Brother machine,
I engaged both part buttons.

13
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.140
Knit across.

14
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.040
Notice that the needles that you pulled
have loops and the needles that remained

15
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:54.720
in working position slipped.
The yarn passed under those needles.

16
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.460
Next, pull those slipped
needles all the way out.

17
00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.700
Let's call these the odd needles,

18
00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:07.340
with the carriage still
set to slip, knit across.

19
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.600
This time, the odd needles will have

20
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:17.400
a loop and the even needles form a loose,
somewhat sloppy stitch.

21
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.180
We'll do this again.

22
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:26.850
Pull the original, even needles
all the way out. Pass the carriage.

23
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.960
Again, the odd needles will slip.

24
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:34.900
Finally, pull the odd needles out.

25
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:42.260
When you knit, those odd needles will form
sloppy stitches and the even needles slip.

26
00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.720
Continue knitting, and you'll see that you

27
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:51.080
have a nice closed cast-on that won't run,
but you did it in very few steps.

28
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.560
If you struggle with this, consider
using a cast-on comb on that first row.

29
00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.360
It may help form
the stitches more cleanly.

